{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 Calibri;}}\f0\fs24 Reordering Mint Seeds: Which Reference Details to Check First \u183? Questions and answers\par A mint reorder often begins with a short line from last season's order note, and that shortcut can hide more than one plant. The catalogue behind this guide lists four separate mint species records, so a repeat order is only safe when the written reference matches the botanical record. The notes below answer the practical questions that come up when checking a mint line before sending a new enquiry.\par \b Why is "mint" not enough to repeat an order?\b0\par The mint range carries Mentha spicata, Mentha longifolia, Mentha peperita and Mentha pulegium as distinct catalogue records, each with its own species entry and its own varieties. A note reading only "mint" could therefore point to a spearmint-type line, horse mint, peppermint or pennyroyal material. The shorter the name on the old note, the more identities it can conceal, which is why the record should be checked before the order is treated as a straight repeat.\par \b Which reference fields should be compared first?\b0\par Start with the species record. The catalogue groups entries under the botanical name and lists common names such as Horse Mint, Peppermint or Brefai alongside, so the species page is the anchor for any comparison. Then confirm the variety inside that species, because a single species page can hold more than one variety. Finally check the SKU, the field that ties an order to one reference. If the old note carries no species, treat the identity as unresolved instead of choosing the closest-sounding common name.\par \b What does a shortened common name usually hide?\b0\par Three problems appear again and again on reorders. A group name stands in for a species, so "mint" describes the whole catalogue group rather than a stock item. A common name appears without its species, so horse mint and peppermint read as related even though they are separate records. A variety drops out of the note, so the species alone no longer identifies the exact reference that was sold. None of this means the earlier order was wrong, only that the note is too vague to repeat safely.\par \b How should units and pricing be handled?\b0\par Price per kilogram and price for a stated number of seeds rest on different bases of comparison, so confirm which unit the original line used before comparing costs across seasons. Pack size, available quantity and final price are confirmed in the quotation. A reorder is an enquiry, not an automatic repeat, and the catalogue guidance for resale enquiries is to state the SKU and the required weight.\par \b What can a catalogue name not prove?\b0\par A catalogue name is not evidence for a named clone or for a food-use statement. When a selection's identity is unclear, ask for clarification rather than filling the gap with an assumption. This matters when a customer asks why two mint lines are listed separately: the accurate answer is that they are different botanical records, not proof of a particular garden or culinary performance.\par \b What is a practical reorder routine?\b0\par Read the previous order note and underline every word that is a group name or a common name without a species. Open the mint species pages and match each line to a species record, then confirm variety and SKU against what was actually supplied. Note which unit the original line used, and send the enquiry with SKU and required weight, asking for clarification on any line that cannot be matched. The routine is slow for the first order and fast afterwards, because the corrected note is reusable next season.\par {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://info0277593.wixsite.com/bulk-seeds-internati/beitrag/reordering-mint-seeds-reference-identity-check"}{\fldrslt Reordering Mint Seeds: Which Reference Details to Check First}}\par {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://bulk-seeds-international.github.io/bulkseedsinternational-en/dateien/mi